// A call used for its value, as in `printf("%d", die())`: its arguments are
// lowered, and when the callee may exit the call becomes a statement of its own
// storing into a temporary, and the block is cut after it. The runner looks at
// the exit status between blocks, so the rest of the expression (the printf)
// never runs once the callee has exited. A call that cannot exit stays in place.
lower_call_val(int e) {
  int r = lower_call(e);
  int hit = mx_hoist(r);
  if(hit == 1) {
    r = hoist_call(e);
  }
} return int r;
